intervals over runs, and section 2.4's rule).

## 1. Memory per idle task (threshold: within 4x Go at 100k and 1M)

Bytes per task = (VmRSS + VmPTE after - before) / N from `/proc/self/status`,
read one second after all N tasks have parked (`--settle-ms 1000`). Shallow use
is about 1 KiB of frames; the excursion is a 32 KiB recursion of 4000-byte
frames that touches every page, after which the task parks again. For Go the
after-excursion figure of record is read after four `runtime.GC()` calls and
`debug.FreeOSMemory()` (policy 4); Java's after `System.gc()`. C's exact size is
its nominal size plus the excursion, as a compiler bounding the whole task body
would choose (4 KiB + 32 KiB = 36 KiB, and so on). Ratio = median over Go's
median on the same machine; ranges in brackets are the five runs' minimum and
maximum, and for ratios the conservative [min/max, max/min] that section 2.4
uses. M2's columns are its smoke run (10k tasks, one run, harness 0.1.0), which
is not at the threshold's N.

{{ table("mem") }}

Section 2.4 of proposal/20 applied to M1 (each stackful configuration's worst
ratio over the four conditions; at 1M after the excursion, where Go's own cell
cannot fit 16 GiB, the larger of the ratios to Go after GC at 100k and to Go
shallow at 1M, a lower bound of Go after GC at 1M):

{{ table("rule") }}

* **The verdict on M1.** D(B) is the only stackful strategy that passes, at
  every reservation, with w = {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} at 1 MiB. B and C keep the
  excursion's pages ("commit creep", {{ im(M1, "B/1M", 100000, "excursion") }}
  KiB per task for B/1 MiB) and fail against Go after its collection
  ({{ im(M1, "go", 100000, "excursion_after_gc") }} KiB), although measured against Go
  before its collection ({{ im(M1, "go", 100000, "excursion") }} KiB) they are at
  {{ rat(M1, IM, "B/1M N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion") }}x
  (B/1 MiB) and
  {{ rat(M1, IM, "C/36K N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion", "go N=100000", "total_per_task_excursion") }}x
  (C/4 KiB). A and D(A) fail on scale first:
  each guard page splits a mapping, and the process stops at
  {{ med(M1, IM, "A/1M N=100000", "tasks_created", "", 0) }} tasks under
  `vm.max_map_count` 65530 (their per-task figures over the tasks that were
  created match B's and D(B)'s).
* **What a task costs.** D(B)/1 MiB holds
  {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB resident per
  task at 1M (one stack page plus the task record) and
  {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB of page
  tables: a 4 KiB page-table page maps 2 MiB, which two 1 MiB slots share. The
  reservation costs page tables, not resident memory: 128 KiB slots need
  {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/128K N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB, 8 MiB slots
  {{ rng(M1, IM, "D(B)/8M N=1000000", "pte_per_task_shallow", "KiB") }} KiB. Measured on
  RSS alone, as section 6.5 literally defines the resident set, the stackful
  strategies' ratios are smaller still (D(B)/1 MiB at 100k:
  {{ rat(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow", "go N=100000", "rss_per_task_shallow") }}x
  shallow; the RSS-only columns of `summarize.py`), because Go's page tables
  are negligible and theirs are not.
* **Decommit on park.** D(B) made {{ num(notefield(M1, IM, "D(B)/1M N=1000000", "tasks_created", "decommits")) }}
  `madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)` calls for 1M tasks (the `decommits=` field of the
  rows' `note`): one per task, at the park after its excursion. The park after shallow use made none, because the probe
  watermark showed that the stack had not grown below its top page.
* **1M tasks.** Per-task memory does not change from 100k to 1M for any
  stackful strategy. At 1M, A, B, C and Go measured the shallow phase only
  (policy 7): keeping the excursion's pages for 1M tasks needs about
  {{ f"{cell(M1, IM, 'B/1M N=100000').med('total_per_task_excursion') * 1e6 / 2**30:.0f}" }} GiB
  (B/1 MiB's figure at 100k times 1M).
* **C and the 5 KiB reserve.** C's shallow figure is
  {{ im(M1, "C/36K", 100000) }} KiB, against {{ im(M2, "C/36K", 10000) }} KiB in M2's smoke
  run with the earlier 8 KiB reserve. A slot of 36 + 5 KiB is not a whole number
  of pages, so a quarter of the slots have their top frames straddle a page
  boundary and touch two pages. Rounding C's slots to pages (the reserve
  absorbing the rounding) would restore the earlier figure; C does not pass
  either way.
* **The controls.** Java's virtual threads copy a parked thread's frames into
  heap chunks, so Java's figures ({{ im(M1, "java", 1000000) }} KiB shallow at 1M) are a
  managed baseline, not a non-moving-stack precedent. E and tokio keep no stack
  per task ({{ im(M1, "E", 1000000) }} and {{ im(M1, "tokio", 1000000) }} KiB). On M2, Go's
  before-GC figure after the excursion is {{ im(M2, "go", 10000, "excursion") }} KiB
  (transparent huge pages are `always` there); after GC it is
  {{ im(M2, "go", 10000, "excursion_after_gc") }} KiB, as on M1.
* **M2.** The smoke run's 10k-task ratios agree with M1's to within a few
  hundredths for A, B, D(A) and D(B) (D(B)/1 MiB {{ imr(M2, "D(B)/1M", 10000) }}x and
  {{ imr(M2, "D(B)/1M", 10000, "excursion") }}x against M1's
  {{ imr(M1, "D(B)/1M", 100000) }}x and {{ imr(M1, "D(B)/1M", 100000, "excursion") }}x at
  100k); C's differ because its slot geometry changed with the reserve (above).
  10k is not a threshold N, so they are supporting evidence only.

## 2. Spawn plus join (threshold: median within 3x Go with pooled stacks)

100k spawn-plus-join pairs after 10k unrecorded pairs (Java: 20k, for its JIT),
each timed with a monotonic clock; the trivial task returns at once.

{{ table("spawn_join") }}

* **Verdict.** B's median is {{ rat(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }} times Go's on
  M1 ({{ ci(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}) and {{ rat(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }} times on M2's smoke run:
  within 3x on both machines (**holds**). D(B), the candidate, costs about
  what B costs ({{ rat(M1, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x): a task that never grew
  below its top page is released without a system call.
* **The cost of mapping.** A maps, protects and unmaps a stack per
  task ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "A/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us); with a cache of 1024 freed
  stacks it costs what B costs ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "A/1M stack_cache=1024", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }}
  us). D(A) with the cache still pays one `madvise` per release
  ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "D(A)/1M stack_cache=1024", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us), which D(B)'s
  watermark avoids.
* **Tails.** B's p99 ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p99", "us") }} us) is below Go's
  ({{ rng(M1, SJ, "go", "spawn_join_p99", "us") }} us) on M1. Java's virtual threads cost
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "java", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us per spawn and join after 20,000
  unrecorded rounds for the JIT.

## 3. Scale: 1M idle tasks on a 16 GiB machine with default sysctls

`max-tasks --tasks 1000000 --mem-guard 512M --hold-secs 60`: tasks that do
shallow work and park are spawned one at a time until the cap, a failed spawn,
or less than 512 MiB of memory headroom (`MemAvailable`, or the memory cgroup's
headroom when smaller); a run that reaches the cap then keeps every task parked
for 60 seconds, checks that all of them are still parked, and reads memory
again (section 6.5: "pass if all park and the process survives 60 seconds").
OS threads have a cap of 30,000.

{{ table("max_tasks") }}

* A and D(A) stop at {{ med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks with
  {{ med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "vmas_at_stop", "", 0) }} mappings: every task's guard
  page splits its stack mapping, so the process meets `vm.max_map_count`
  (65530) at about 32k tasks. That is the scale row's fail condition:
  **guard-page-per-task is out** as a default, which policy 3 already concludes.
* B, C, D(B), E and tokio, and the Go and Java controls, all park 1M tasks; after
  the 60-second hold every task is still parked, and per-task memory is
  unchanged (B/1 MiB {{ rng(M1, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_at_stop", "KiB") }} KiB
  at the stop and {{ rng(M1, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "total_per_task_after_hold", "KiB") }} KiB
  after the hold). The pooled strategies' reservations do not approach the
  mapping limit: B/1 MiB has {{ med(M1, MT, "B/1M N=1000000", "vmas_at_stop", "", 0) }}
  mappings in all at 1M tasks, with about
  {{ f"{int(notefield(M1, IM, 'B/1M N=1000000', 'tasks_created', 'reserved_bytes')) / (1 << 40):.2f}" }}
  TiB of address space reserved in 1 GiB chunks (`reserved_bytes` in the
  `idle-memory` rows' note).
* OS threads stop at {{ med(M1, MT, "os-threads N=30000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }}
  ({{ ", ".join(sorted({n for st, n in cell(M1, MT, "os-threads N=30000").status["limit"]})) }}):
  each Rust thread maps its stack, its guard, and the signal stack that `std`
  maps with its own guard.
* M2's smoke run had a cap of 100k and the runner image's `vm.max_map_count`
  of 262144, so A reached its cap there; under the kernel default the
  full-scale workflow restores, A meets the same wall.

## 4. Echo (threshold: 10k-connection p99 within 2x Go) and idle connections

`echo`: N closed-loop client tasks in the same process (64-byte messages, a
write and a read of the echo per round trip), 5 s unrecorded then 30 s
recorded; the server is one task per connection. `idle-connections`: N server
tasks parked in `read`, the client ends held as plain sockets, memory read after
30 s idle (section 6.5). Both ends count against `RLIMIT_NOFILE`, so N
connections need 2N + 64 descriptors.

{{ table("echo") }}

* **10k connections were not run on M1.** They need 20,064 descriptors and the
  hard limit is 20000 (policy 3: the cell runs only where the default limit
  allows). The harness and the controls record failed rows instead. M2's full
  run can run them (its hard limit is 65536).
* **At 1000 connections** B's p99 is {{ rat(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}
  times Go's ({{ ci(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}) and
  D(B)'s {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}
  ({{ ci(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}); D(A) is the
  exception at {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}
  ({{ ci(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}):
  without a watermark it makes a `madvise` call at every park, and every
  request parks its server task and its client task. I/O design, for the
  record: taskbench's workers poll one shared epoll instance (busy workers every
  31 scheduling decisions, idle ones before they spin) and one sleeping worker
  blocks in it; Go uses its integrated netpoller. A miss would be "runtime
  design review, not a language change".
* **Idle connections.** At 9,900 connections a pooled strategy's idle connection
  costs {{ rng(M1, IC, "B/1M connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }} KiB
  (B/1 MiB; {{ rng(M1, IC, "C/8K connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }} KiB for
  C/8 KiB, whose small slots need fewer page tables), against Go's
  {{ rng(M1, IC, "go connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "KiB") }} KiB; the server task
  holds a 4 KiB read buffer on its stack.

## 5. Stack exhaustion (threshold: no segfault, ever)

`deep-recursion --framed` in every stackful configuration (E and tokio run the
parse and the naive recursion on the worker's 8 MiB stack, plus a recursion of
boxed futures): a 10k-deep recursive-descent parse (four frames per level), a
1M-deep naive recursion with a drop guard every 256 frames, and the framed
recursion (a 256-byte local and a drop guard in every frame), each in its own
task, then a task that checks the runtime still works. The reserve columns are
the bytes of the 5 KiB probe reserve the unwind overwrote (the reserve is
painted before the case and scanned after), the maximum over five runs.

{{ table("deep") }}

* **Every exhaustion is clean**: {{ sum(1 for k, c in cells.items() if k[0] == M1 and k[1] == DR for m in ("parse_outcome", "naive_outcome", "framed_outcome", "naive-async_outcome") for st, n in c.status.get(m, []) if st == "ok") }}
  outcome rows on M1, none unclean, and the follow-up task ran in every run.
  `process_exit` rows (crashes) across every CSV of both results directories:
  **{{ sum(1 for k, c in cells.items() for st in c.status.get("process_exit", [])) }}**.
* **Mechanism.** B, C and D(B) fail through the software probe and unwind, so
  every destructor runs (0 drop guards skipped). A and D(A) fail through the
  guard page; the trap handler ends the task without unwinding, so the frames'
  destructors are skipped ({{ med(M1, DR, "A/1M", "naive_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0) }} drop
  guards in the naive recursion at 1 MiB,
  {{ med(M1, DR, "A/1M", "framed_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0) }} in the framed one).
* **The probe reserve.** The largest unwind on M1 used
  {{ f"{max(int(max(cell(M1, DR, l).values[m])) for l in ['B/128K', 'B/1M', 'B/8M', 'C/2K', 'C/8K', 'C/16K', 'D(B)/128K', 'D(B)/1M', 'D(B)/8M'] for m in ['parse_probe_reserve_used', 'naive_probe_reserve_used', 'framed_probe_reserve_used'] if cell(M1, DR, l).values.get(m)):,}" }}
  bytes of the 5,120-byte reserve. Before the fix of section 10.3, the first
  unwind of each process used about 5 KiB, and M2's smoke run shows that
  pattern ({{ vmax(M2, DR, "B/1M", "parse_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} bytes for the first,
  {{ vmax(M2, DR, "B/1M", "naive_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} for the next).
* **Depth per reservation.** The 10k-deep parse completes only at 8 MiB (and on
  E and tokio, whose worker stacks are 8 MiB); at 1 MiB it fails cleanly at
  level {{ med(M1, DR, "B/1M", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0) }}. B's usable stack is a
  little over its reservation (the slot is rounded up to a page above the 5 KiB
  reserve), so B reaches a few more levels than A.
* The corpus-wide `--stats stack-depth` histogram (interpreter era) is reported
  separately and decides whether the default reservation suffices.

## 6. Starvation (threshold: probe p99 no worse than Go and under 10 ms)

Four compute tasks per worker (16 on M1's four workers) run an LCG loop and
reach a yield point every K iterations, the stand-in for a compiler-inserted
yield check; a probe task sleeps 1 ms in a loop and records how late each
wake-up is, first alone (1 s) and then under the load (11 s). Go runs the same
loop with no yields at all and relies on its default asynchronous preemption
(policy 8); Java has no preemption of virtual threads and runs with no yields
and with `Thread.yield()` every K iterations. Labels: the default K is 10000;
`yield_every=0` means no yield points.

{{ table("starvation") }}

* **With yield points the probe's p99 is far below Go's**:
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us at K = 10k (slices of
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) and
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us at K = 100k,
  against {{ rng(M1, ST, "go yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms for Go's
  preemption; the threshold holds for every strategy. M2's smoke run agrees
  (B {{ med(M2, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us, Go
  {{ med(M2, ST, "go yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms, with 4 compute tasks).
* **Without yield points** (`yield_every=0`) the probe cannot run until the
  compute tasks stop: {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms.
  Policy 8 expects this and reports it without counting it.
* **What yield points cost.** Compute throughput with no yield points is
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }} iterations per
  second. Relative to that median, the median throughput with a yield point
  every 100k iterations is {{ f"{(cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=100000').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') - 1) * 100:+.1f}" }} %, every 10k
  {{ f"{(cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') - 1) * 100:+.1f}" }} %, and every 1k {{ f"{(cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=1000').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') - 1) * 100:+.1f}" }} %. The five-run ranges
  (the one above, and {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "compute_iterations_per_sec", "", 0) }} at 10k)
  are wider than the first two differences, so at 10k and 100k iterations the cost is below what
  this machine resolves; at 1k (slices of
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=1000", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) it is clear.
  The cost is the switches themselves: the check on the loop's back-edge is the
  `loop` workload of section 7 and costs almost nothing. Because policy 8 puts
  yield points in the 3 % budget, the yield interval is a design parameter: on
  this machine, slices of about 0.2 ms (K = 100k) cost nothing measurable and
  keep the probe's p99 at {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }}
  us, {{ f"{cell(M1, ST, 'go yield_every=0').med('probe_loaded_late_p99') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=100000').med('probe_loaded_late_p99'):.0f}" }}
  times lower than Go's.
* tokio's p99 of about 1 ms is its timer's granularity, not starvation. OS
  threads rely on the kernel's preemption.

## 7. Software probe overhead (threshold: at most 3 % on compute loops)

Each workload is compiled without probes and with three probe models, and the
four variants run alternately 31 times inside one task on the strategy's stack;
a cell's value is the ratio of medians minus one, and the table shows the
median and range over five processes. `split` is the model of record: `cmp rsp,
fs:[0x70]; jb`, the prologue check of GCC's `-fsplit-stack`, against a slot the
scheduler keeps equal to the running task's limit. `arg` passes the limit as an
argument; `tls` reads a Rust thread-local (not migration-safe). `loop` is the
call-free LCG loop of section 6 with the same check on every back-edge: the
cost model of a compiler-inserted yield check, which policy 8 counts in this
budget (in the other workloads the entry check can double as the yield point,
as Go's prologue check did before asynchronous preemption).

{{ table("probe") }}

* **Taken at face value, two of the three registered workloads exceed 3 %.**
  `fib` does in every run on every strategy (B:
  {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %; the lowest single run of
  any strategy, {{ f"{min(min(cell(M1, PO, l).values['fib_split_probe_overhead']) for l in ['A/1M', 'B/1M', 'C/8K', 'D(B)/1M', 'E']):.1f}" }} %),
  and `tree`'s median does on every strategy (B:
  {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %), although single runs of
  `tree` fall below it; `calls` is within it
  ({{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "calls_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %), and so is the back-edge
  yield check ({{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %, because the
  compare and branch run in parallel with the loop's multiply chain). M2's
  smoke run (harness 0.1.0 built with rustc 1.99, on an AMD EPYC 7763) measured `fib`
  {{ med(M2, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % and `tree`
  {{ med(M2, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %.
* **Why this does not decide the bar.** `fib` is an upper bound by
  construction: LLVM turns one of its two recursive calls into a loop that keeps
  the probe written at the top of the source function, so the check runs about
  twice per machine-level call, which a compiler inserting it into the prologue
  after optimisation would not do. `calls`' callee is a small leaf that a
  compiler would exempt (Go's `NOSPLIT` rule); `tree` is a fair case. And there
  are no hardware counters ("{{ note(M1, PO, "B/1M", "instruction_counts") }}" on M1),
  so only wall-clock medians exist: between the five processes of B's cell the
  without-probes time of `fib` moved by
  {{ f"{(max(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['fib_without_probes']) / min(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['fib_without_probes']) - 1) * 100:.1f}" }} %
  and that of `tree` by
  {{ f"{(max(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['tree_without_probes']) / min(cell(M1, PO, 'B/1M').values['tree_without_probes']) - 1) * 100:.1f}" }} %.
  The owner's policy 1 therefore reports the bar as measured and leaves the
  decision to the native era, on a machine with a PMU and a fixed frequency,
  with Sisu's own probe placement. The measurements are a warning for that
  decision, not a pass.
* **Not measured:** a vectorisable loop. A check inserted before vectorisation
  would prevent it, so a compiler must place yield checks after loop
  optimisation (per vector iteration, or every N iterations).

## 8. io_uring cancellation into stack buffers (amendment A1, D-058)

Two procedures per stackful strategy, five runs each, 1M reads per run in
batches of 8,192 in flight. `pipe_mix=cancel` is A1 as registered: every read
targets an empty pipe, so it stays in flight, and every one is cancelled through
its task; the cancelled call submits `IORING_OP_ASYNC_CANCEL` and returns only on
the read's own completion; afterwards late data is written into every pipe, so
any read the kernel still held would land, and every buffer (4 KiB, on the
task's stack, the frame kept alive until the batch ends) is checked again. The
default race mix (512-byte buffers) writes data for a quarter of the reads,
cancels a quarter, and does both, in either order, for half. B also runs the
file-backed variants: `O_DIRECT` reads cancelled while the device holds them
(`-ENOENT`, and a later completion), and buffered reads with `IOSQE_ASYNC`
(`-EALREADY`). `--break-contract` is the negative control: a cancelled task
stops waiting at once.

{{ table("uring") }}

* **Zero, as required.** No byte of any buffer changed after its call returned,
  in any run of either procedure on A, B, C, D(A) and D(B), nor in the file
  variants: {{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M + ["B/1M source=file", "B/1M source=file-buffered"], "buffer_writes_after_return") }}
  bytes over {{ runs_of(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M + ["B/1M source=file", "B/1M source=file-buffered"], "buffer_writes_after_return") }}
  runs. The negative control writes
  {{ total(M1, CU, "B/1M break_contract=true", "buffer_writes_after_return") }} bytes over
  its five runs, so the check sees what it is meant to see.
* **A cancel that cannot stop the read.** A quarter of the reads carry
  `IOSQE_ASYNC`, which sends them to an io-wq worker thread, where a blocked
  pipe read can only be interrupted. A cancel that finds such a read already
  executing answers `-EALREADY`
  ({{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "cancel_ealready") }}
  times in the {{ runs_of(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "cancel_ealready") }}
  pipe runs above), and the read usually ends at once. In the first
  full-scale group, {{ superseded_stuck()[0] }} of
  {{ f"{superseded_stuck()[4]:,}" }} pipe reads did not: each stayed blocked
  until the harness wrote its late data at the round's 30-second deadline, and
  each call returned only after that completion,
  {{ f"{superseded_stuck()[1] / 1e3:.3f}" }}-{{ f"{superseded_stuck()[2] / 1e3:.3f}" }} s after
  its cancel (the slowest cancellation of each of the {{ superseded_stuck()[3] }} runs
  concerned), with its buffer intact afterwards. In the rerun above,
  {{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "completed_with_late_data") }}
  reads did (`completed_with_late_data`). Waiting is what keeps a lent buffer
  safe, and D-058 requires it; the consequence is that a cancelled task waits as
  long as an uncancellable read does. That is a liveness question for the native
  runtime (re-issuing the cancel signals the worker again; pollable files can use
  poll-armed reads, which a cancel always stops), not a memory-safety one, and it
  reopens nothing. The first group's check counted these reads' late data as
  "written at return"; section 10.3 describes the fix and where the superseded
  rows are kept.
* **The case D-058 exists for** is the file-backed one: a cancel that finds the
  read already issued to the device (`-ENOENT`) while the read still completes
  later ({{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M source=file", "completed_after_failed_cancel", "", 0) }}
  reads per run); the call waited for those completions.
* **Cancel-to-return latency** in the registered procedure is
  {{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M pipe_mix=cancel", "cancel_to_return_p50", "ms") }} ms median and
  {{ rng(M1, CU, "B/1M pipe_mix=cancel", "cancel_to_return_p99", "ms") }} ms p99 for B: the
  harness cancels 8,192 reads in a burst, and each cancellation queues one
  request to the ring's thread, so the figure is dominated by the burst, not by
  one cancellation. This group ran before the quiet gate existed (section
  10.2), so these latencies may include other work's CPU use; they are not a
  verdict input.

## 9. Verdict and Q-6 recommendation

### 9.1 Verdict per bet-3 row (harness era; provisional, see the status note)

Section 2.1 of proposal/20: **holds** means the threshold is met with the
pre-specified effect; **fails** means it is missed or a guard trips; anything
else, including too little data or machines that disagree, is **not shown**,
and no fallback is built on it. Section 7.8: only the primary (memory) decides
the bet, the guard (exhaustion in ordinary code) can only stop it, and each
secondary triggers its own action.

| Row | Role | Verdict | The numbers | Action (section 8.3) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Memory per idle task | primary | **not shown** | M1: D(B) passes, w = {{ w(M1, "D(B)/128K") }} / {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} / {{ w(M1, "D(B)/8M") }} at 128 KiB / 1 MiB / 8 MiB, every five-run range far from 4; B (w = {{ imr(M1, "B/8M", 100000, "excursion") }} at 8 MiB, {{ imr(M1, "B/1M", 100000, "excursion") }} at 1 MiB) and C ({{ imr(M1, "C/36K", 100000, "excursion") }}) fail after the excursion; A and D(A) fail at the VMA limit. M2: no cell at 100k or 1M (its 10k smoke ratios agree with M1's) | none yet. If M2's full run also has D(B) at or below 4, the row holds and the "holds at week 8" branch applies; if the machines disagree, the "not shown" branch (no fallback; Q-6 stays open) |
| Stack exhaustion | guard | harness part **holds**; the guard is not decided | every exhaustion on every strategy and reservation is a clean "stack exhausted", five runs each; the runtime works afterwards; {{ sum(1 for k, c in cells.items() if k[0] == M1 for st in c.status.get("process_exit", [])) }} crashes; the probe reserve's need is at most {{ f"{max(int(max(cell(M1, DR, l).values[m])) for l in ['B/128K', 'B/1M', 'B/8M', 'C/2K', 'C/8K', 'C/16K', 'D(B)/128K', 'D(B)/1M', 'D(B)/8M'] for m in ['parse_probe_reserve_used', 'naive_probe_reserve_used', 'framed_probe_reserve_used'] if cell(M1, DR, l).values.get(m)):,}" }} bytes of its 5,120 | the corpus `--stats stack-depth` histogram decides at week 40 |
| Spawn plus join | secondary | **holds** | B {{ rat(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x Go on M1 ({{ ci(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}), {{ rat(M2, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}x on M2 (smoke) | no design review |
| Scale: 1M idle tasks, 16 GiB, default sysctls | secondary | **fails for A and D(A)**; holds for B, C and D(B) | A and D(A) stop at {{ med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks; B, C, D(B), E and tokio, and Go and Java, park 1M tasks and keep all of them parked through the 60-second hold | "fails on creation cost or VMA limits only": pooled stacks with software probes become the design. Policy 5 already makes D(B) the default, and this run is the rerun on that strategy |
| Probe overhead (part of the scale row) | secondary | **not decided** (policy 1) | `fib` {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % (an upper bound), `tree` {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %, `calls` {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "calls_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} %, back-edge yield check {{ rng(M1, PO, "B/1M", "loop_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % (B); at face value above 3 % for `fib` and `tree` | the native era, on a machine with a PMU and a fixed frequency, with Sisu's own probe placement |
| Echo p99 at 10k connections | secondary | **not shown** | not run on M1 (descriptor limit); at 1000 connections B {{ rat(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x, D(B) {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x and D(A) {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}x Go | none; M2's full run includes the 10k echo (its hard limit is 65536) |
| Idle RSS at 100k connections | reported | (no threshold) | at 9,900: D(B) {{ rat(M1, IC, "D(B)/1M connections=9900", "total_per_connection", "go connections=9900") }}x Go | none |
| Starvation | secondary | **holds** with compiler-inserted yield points | B's p99 {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us (K = 10k) and {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us (K = 100k) against Go's {{ rng(M1, ST, "go yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms, all under 10 ms; without yield points the probe waits the whole run ({{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=0", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "ms") }} ms), reported and not counted (policy 8) | asynchronous preemption is not revisited |
| Cancellation with a lent buffer (A1) | secondary | **holds** on M1 | {{ sumcells(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "buffer_writes_after_return") }} bytes written after return in {{ runs_of(M1, CU, [x + " pipe_mix=cancel" for x in STACKFUL_1M] + STACKFUL_1M, "buffer_writes_after_return") }} runs on the five stackful strategies, 1M reads per run; the negative control detects its violations ({{ total(M1, CU, "B/1M break_contract=true", "buffer_writes_after_return") }} bytes) | none; D-058 stands |
| Semantics, `sim` study | secondary | not run | interpreter era | Phases 6-9 |

**Bet 3 at week 8: not shown, pending M2.** The primary has one machine's
evidence, and on it the default candidate's worst ratio is {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }} at
1 MiB against a bar of 4; none of the bet's fail branches is triggered, so no
fallback is built. The guard is decided at week 40.

### 9.2 Q-6 recommendation (provisional, harness era)

* **Strategy: D(B)**, pooled stacks carved from large reservations, with
  software probes and decommit on park, the probe doubling as the decommit
  watermark. It is the only stackful strategy that passes the memory primary on
  M1; B and C fail it by commit creep (an excursion's pages stay resident), and
  A and D(A) cannot create 100k tasks under default sysctls. Its cost over B is
  within the noise: {{ rng(M1, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} against
  {{ rng(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us per spawn plus join, and echo p99
  {{ rng(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "ms") }} against
  {{ rng(M1, EC, "B/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "ms") }} ms. Decommit is needed,
  and its system call shows only where it is paid on every park: D(A) has no
  watermark and makes one `madvise` per park, and its echo throughput is
  {{ rng(M1, EC, "D(A)/1M connections=1000", "echo_throughput", "", 0) }} requests per second
  against D(B)'s {{ rng(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_throughput", "", 0) }}.
  In `idle-memory`, D(B) made one call per task, at the park after its
  excursion, and none at the park after shallow use.
* **Default reservation: 1 MiB.** Under D(B) a reservation costs page tables,
  not resident memory: {{ w(M1, "D(B)/128K") }}x, {{ w(M1, "D(B)/1M") }}x and
  {{ w(M1, "D(B)/8M") }}x Go (worst condition) at 128 KiB, 1 MiB and 8 MiB. 1 MiB
  keeps a margin under the bar for the runtime's growth, is the size of the
  JVM's default thread stack, and runs
  {{ med(M1, DR, "D(B)/1M", "naive_depth_reached", "", 0) }} levels of a small recursion
  and {{ med(M1, DR, "D(B)/1M", "parse_depth_reached", "", 0) }} levels of the four-frame
  parser before a clean failure. 8 MiB is admissible on memory (it completes the
  10k-deep parse) and is the step to take if the corpus histogram finds
  ordinary programs deeper than 1 MiB; above 8 MiB, memory must be re-measured
  (the plan's exhaustion row).
* **Probes, not guard pages.** One guard page per task stops at
  {{ med(M1, MT, "A/1M N=1000000", "max_tasks", "", 0) }} tasks under the default
  `vm.max_map_count`, and a guard-page trap discards the exhausted task's frames
  without running their destructors ({{ med(M1, DR, "A/1M", "naive_leaked_drop_guards", "", 0) }}
  drop guards skipped in the 1M-deep recursion at 1 MiB, none under probes).
  With probes every exhaustion unwinds cleanly. The plan attaches a condition
  to this choice, probe overhead of at most 3 % on compute loops, and that
  condition is open: policy 1 leaves it to the native era, and at face value
  `fib` and `tree` exceed it here (section 7). Two further conditions come with
  probes:
  the runtime must resolve the unwinder's lazily bound symbols before tasks run
  (or link them eagerly), which this phase found and fixed (section 10.3); and
  the native compiler's probe should compare the stack pointer minus the frame
  size before allocating the frame, so that the reserve covers only the unwinder
  and unprobed callees. With both, the policy's 5 KiB (5,120-byte) reserve
  leaves {{ f"{5120 - max(int(max(cell(M1, DR, l).values[m])) for l in ['B/128K', 'B/1M', 'B/8M', 'C/2K', 'C/8K', 'C/16K', 'D(B)/128K', 'D(B)/1M', 'D(B)/8M'] for m in ['parse_probe_reserve_used', 'naive_probe_reserve_used', 'framed_probe_reserve_used'] if cell(M1, DR, l).values.get(m)):,}" }}
  bytes over the largest need measured on M1.
* **`spawn` stays ambient**, without a capability. The research found no
  supply-chain attack that gating `spawn` would have stopped, and nothing in the
  harness argues otherwise: a task costs {{ rng(M1, SJ, "D(B)/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "us") }} us
  to spawn and join and {{ im(M1, "D(B)/1M", 1000000) }} KiB at rest, and the risk of
  code that spawns too many is a resource limit for the runtime to enforce (the
  scale row's guards), not an authority to grant.
* **Yield checks (Q-34)**, decided as compiler-inserted, need an interval:
  yielding every 100k iterations of the test loop (slices of about
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) held the probe's
  p99 at {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M yield_every=100000", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us, and
  every 10k (about {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "slice_between_yields", "us") }} us) at
  {{ rng(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us, both at a throughput within
  the five runs' spread of the loop without yield points (median changes of
  {{ f"{(cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=100000').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') - 1) * 100:+.1f}" }} % and {{ f"{(cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') - 1) * 100:+.1f}" }} %); every 1k iterations cost
  {{ f"{(1 - cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=1000').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec')) * 100:.1f}" }} %.
  The check on the back-edge is nearly free (section 7); the switches are the
  cost, and it falls with the slice. A slice of a few hundred microseconds meets
  the starvation row by two orders of magnitude, and the interval should be
  chosen to fit what the probes leave of the 3 % budget, which the native era
  measures.
* **The stackless fallback is not needed** on this evidence: the default
  candidate passes the memory primary on M1 with a wide margin and every
  exhaustion is clean. Its cost stays on file
  ([`stackless-fallback-estimate.md`](stackless-fallback-estimate.md): 17-21
  engineer-weeks, plus 3 if frames become values), for the two cases that would
  call for it: M2's full run contradicting M1, or the corpus histogram (week 40)
  finding ordinary depth beyond 8 MiB. The yield-point frame-boundary question is
  therefore not flagged to Phase 3.

### 9.3 What the harness era cannot settle

* **The 3 % probe budget.** Neither machine has hardware counters, and run to run
  the wall-clock overhead moves by more than the bar (section 7). The decision
  waits for the native era on bare metal.
* **The default reservation against real programs.** The harness shows what each
  size costs and how deep it goes; whether ordinary bet-1 and bet-2 programs fit
  1 MiB is the interpreter era's histogram.
* **Sisu's own frames.** Every number here is Rust code on native stacks. Frame
  sizes, where the compiler places probes and yield checks (before allocating a
  frame; on which back-edges; before or after vectorisation), how the unwinder
  or the runtime's own unwinding behaves, and what a Sisu task's record costs
  change these figures. The native era reruns this harness on Sisu frames, and
  only then is Q-6 closed finally (18: "closed provisionally with the harness
  era label ..., and finally when the native backend reruns the harness on Sisu
  frames").
* **How long a cancelled call may wait.** When a cancel cannot stop a read
  (`-EALREADY`), D-058 makes the call wait for the read's own completion, which
  keeps the buffer safe and can take as long as the I/O (section 8). Bounding
  that wait (re-issuing the cancel, poll-armed reads for pollable files) is a
  design question for the native runtime that the harness only observed.
* **The registered echo and connection cells**: 10k connections, 100k idle
  connections, an open-loop load generator on disjoint cores. None was run here.
* **The second machine at full scale**, and a second machine with 16 GB.

## 10. Deviations, amendments, gaps

### 10.1 What the owner's policies changed relative to the plan

The plan (18, Phase 1) asked for "a report with every configuration, not the
best one, reproduced on a second machine by the reviewer", and its threshold
row for probe overhead presupposes machines with hardware counters and a fixed
frequency; the pre-registration's section 4.4 asks for two machines with at
least 16 GiB, default sysctls, the scale test in a 16 GiB memory cgroup, and M2
run by the reviewer. The owner's decisions of October 2026 (README, "Policy
decisions") changed that, and this report follows them:

| Policy | The plan's original wording | What this report does instead |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Machines | two machines, the second the reviewer's, implicitly bare metal | M1 is a 4-vCPU KVM virtual machine without a PMU and with an unpinned frequency; M2 is a GitHub-hosted runner, driven by a workflow rather than by a reviewer. The 3 % probe bar is reported with its noise and not decided (section 7) |
| 2. Context switch | "an assembly context switch from an existing library" | corosensei 0.3.x, unchanged since the harness was built |
| 3. Sysctls and limits | default sysctls; the earlier full-scale script needed `RLIMIT_NOFILE` of 200,064 | default sysctls on M1. On M2 the new workflow restores the kernel default `vm.max_map_count=65530`, which the runner image raises to 262144 (the smoke run used here ran under 262144, so A reached its 100k cap there). The 10k echo and the 100k idle connections run only where the default hard limit allows; on M1 (hard limit 20000) the 10k echo is a failed row and idle connections ran at 9,900 |
| 4. Go after the excursion | "within 4x Go", the Go figure unspecified | Go after four `runtime.GC()` and `FreeOSMemory` |
| 5. Default strategy | none named | D(B) |
| 6. Probe reserve | the harness defaults of 16 KiB (B) and 8 KiB (C) | 5 KiB for B, C and D(B), now the harness default (version 0.2.0) |
| 7. 1M tasks after the excursion | every strategy | only D(A) and D(B); A, B, C and Go measure the shallow phase at 1M (`--skip-excursion`), and their cell reads "not run (policy 7)" |
| 8. Yield points | compute tasks "that yield every K iterations", Go with or without `Gosched` | compiler-inserted yield points every K = 10k and 100k iterations (and 1k); the baseline is Go with its default asynchronous preemption only; no yield points at all is reported, not counted |

### 10.2 Deviations from the harness protocol (proposal/20, section 6.5)

* **Machine 2 has no full-scale data yet.** M2's numbers are the CI smoke run
  of 2026-10-13: 2 workers, one run per cell, harness 0.1.0 (the 16 KiB and
  8 KiB reserves, no unwinder warm-up), `vm.max_map_count` 262144, 3 GiB of
  swap, and no Java (the runner's default JDK is 17). The workflow
  `.github/workflows/harness-full.yml` produces the full-scale M2 run when a
  `harness-run-*` tag is pushed; it installs JDK 21 and Go 1.24, restores the
  kernel's `vm.max_map_count`, turns swap off, sets transparent huge pages to
  `madvise` as on M1 (under the image's `always`, Go's goroutines held
  {{ im(M2, "go", 10000, "excursion") }} KiB each after the excursion, which on an 8 GB
  runner would push Go's 100k cell into the memory guard), and uploads
  `results/github-runner/` as `taskbench-full-<tag>`.
* **M2 is smaller than the plan's machine.** This repository is private, and
  GitHub's `ubuntu-latest` runner for private repositories has 2 vCPUs and
  8 GB (the smoke run's `machine-spec.csv`: 8,323,969,024 bytes); the
  4-vCPU, 16 GB runner is for public repositories. On it the 1M-task cells
  stop at the memory guard and are recorded as failed rows, so M2 can
  reproduce every cell at 100k and below, but not the scale row or the 1M
  memory cells, unless the workflow runs on a 16 GB runner.
* **M1's memory limit** is the sandbox's memory cgroup of 13.4 GiB on a
  15.7 GiB VM, not a 16 GiB cgroup on a machine of at least 16 GiB.
* **M1 is shared.** Other development work (compilations and tests elsewhere in
  the repository) ran on the VM during the runs, at times that were not
  recorded; section 6.5 assumes a machine that runs nothing else. The first
  runs of the four groups that measure time (spawn-join, starvation,
  probe-overhead, echo) were exposed to it: a listing of the compiler
  workspace's build directory made during the echo group showed files written
  in every ten-minute interval from 21:30 to 22:09 and from 22:20 to 22:43 UTC,
  which covers parts of the starvation, probe-overhead and echo groups (nothing
  is known about spawn-join's half minute at 19:54), and in the starvation
  group the first of B's five runs at K = 10k reached
  {{ num(min(float(r['value']) for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-before-quiet-gate/starvation.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'compute_iterations_per_sec' and r['strategy'] == 'B' and 'yield_every=10000;' in r['params'])) }}
  iterations per second against a median of
  {{ num(__import__('statistics').median(float(r['value']) for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-before-quiet-gate/starvation.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'compute_iterations_per_sec' and r['strategy'] == 'B' and 'yield_every=10000;' in r['params'])) }}.
  Those four groups were run again with the quiet gate of `load.sh`
  (`--quiet-below 0.3`, section 11), and only the gated runs are reported; the
  first runs are kept in `superseded-before-quiet-gate/`. The memory, scale and
  correctness groups (deep-recursion, idle-memory, max-tasks, cancel-uring) were
  not run again: what they report does not depend on other processes' CPU use,
  apart from incidental timings (cancellation latencies, spawn rates) that are
  not verdict inputs. A first gated run of spawn-join was stopped after two
  repetitions because `load.sh` then left interrupt and softirq time out of the
  machine's busy time, which this kernel's tick accounting charges to the
  interrupted process, so a competing process would have been invisible during
  the echo benchmark; its rows are in `aborted-gated-rerun/`. The gated runs
  changed no verdict: B's spawn-join median is
  {{ rat(M1, SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }} times Go's (first runs:
  {{ srat(SJ, "B/1M", "spawn_join_p50", "go") }}), B's starvation p99 at K = 10k
  {{ med(M1, ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }} us (first runs:
  {{ smed(ST, "B/1M", "probe_loaded_late_p99", "us") }}), B's `fib` and `tree` probe
  overheads {{ med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % and
  {{ med(M1, PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} % (first runs:
  {{ smed(PO, "B/1M", "fib_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }} and
  {{ smed(PO, "B/1M", "tree_split_probe_overhead", "", 1) }}), and D(B)'s echo p99 at
  1000 connections {{ rat(M1, EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}
  times Go's (first runs:
  {{ srat(EC, "D(B)/1M connections=1000", "echo_rtt_p99", "go connections=1000") }}). What moved
  most is the throughput cost of yield points every 10k iterations,
  {{ f"{(1 - cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / cell(M1, ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec')) * 100:.1f}" }} %
  against {{ f"{(1 - scell(ST, 'B/1M').med('compute_iterations_per_sec') / scell(ST, 'B/1M yield_every=0').med('compute_iterations_per_sec')) * 100:.1f}" }} %
  in the first runs (section 6).
* **Workers** are the VM's vCPUs (4 on M1, 2 on M2); section 4.4's default
  is one per physical core, which a VM does not expose.
* **Memory** is resident plus page tables (VmRSS + VmPTE from
  `/proc/self/status`), the harness's figure of record since its first
  version; section 6.5 says "resident set" from `/proc/self/smaps_rollup`.
  The RSS-only figures are in the CSVs and in `summarize.py`'s output, and
  every verdict is the same on them. The harness reads its baseline after
  runtime start-up without the 1,000-task warm-up of section 6.5; at most
  the first 1,000 tasks could find warm memory, so the effect is at most
  1 % at 100k and conservative (it raises the per-task figure) for every
  implementation.
* **Echo** is a closed loop with the clients in the same process and on the
  same four vCPUs, measured for 30 s after 5 s of warm-up; section 6.5 asks for
  an open-loop load generator on disjoint cores at a fixed rate, 60 s after
  10 s of warm-up, at 10k connections. The 1000-connection figures are the
  harness's comparison of the runtimes, not the registered measurement.
* **Starvation** runs four compute tasks per worker as section 6.5 asks, for
  11 s, which gives each taskbench run about 10k wake-ups; Go's probe wakes
  much later under load, so its runs have fewer wake-ups (section 6 lists
  them).
* **Probe overhead** is 31 alternating repetitions of every variant inside
  one process, five processes per cell; section 6.5 asks for ten runs of
  each. The `loop` workload (the yield check on a back-edge) is new and is
  not in section 6.5's list.
* **A1** reads into a 4 KiB buffer on the task's stack from a pool of pipes
  that hold no data, cancels every read through its task handle, and checks
  the buffer twice: when the call returns, and again after enough "late"
  data has been written into every pipe for any read the kernel still held to
  complete, with the frame kept alive (and so never reused) until its batch
  of 8,192 ends. Section 6.5 writes a canary after the return and quarantines
  the memory; keeping the frame alive is the same check. "Completions that
  arrived after the call returned" cannot happen by construction in the safe
  API (`read` returns only on the completion) and are counted only in the
  negative control's `abandoned_reads`.
* **Controls.** Go is 1.24.7, not the latest stable release; the JDK is 21.0.12
  (JEP 491's unpinning is 24 and later). The "C or Rust stackful coroutine
  library" control is strategy A itself (corosensei's own default stack is a
  guarded mapping per coroutine), not a separate library.
* **Reviewer.** No Go/Java reviewer has configured or checked the controls,
  and no referee has applied the thresholds; the verdicts are the runtime
  engineer's reading of the draft rules.

### 10.3 Changes to the harness in this phase (version 0.2.0)

* **Policy defaults.** The probe reserve is 5 KiB for B, C and D(B)
  (`stack::DEFAULT_PROBE_RESERVE`); version 0.2.0 marks every CSV's
  `machine-spec.csv` (`harness_version`).
* **Bug: the io_uring ring at full scale.** `--concurrency 8192` asked the
  kernel for a completion queue of 131,072 entries, above its maximum of
  65,536, so every full-scale `cancel-uring` run would have been a "skipped:
  io_uring unavailable" row. The ring sizes are now clamped to the kernel's
  limits (`rt::uring::ring_sizes`, with unit tests).
* **Bug: the first unwind ran the dynamic linker inside the probe reserve.**
  `libgcc_s` binds its calls into the C library lazily, and the resolver saves
  the full extended register state on the current stack: about 3 KiB on this
  AVX-512 machine. The first stack exhaustion of every process therefore
  needed about 5 KiB of reserve and later ones about 1.8-2.1 KiB; the earlier
  "5 KiB when landing pads run drop code" was this one-time cost, not drop
  code (with `LD_BIND_NOW=1` the first unwind also needs 1.8 KiB). M2's smoke
  run, made before the fix, shows the same pattern with a smaller register
  state: {{ vmax(M2, DR, "B/1M", "parse_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} bytes for the
  first unwind and {{ vmax(M2, DR, "B/1M", "naive_probe_reserve_used", "", 0) }} for the
  next. With the 5 KiB default, the unit test
  `exhaustion_is_a_clean_task_failure_on_every_strategy` overran a B slot's
  reserve, and its canary caught it. The runtime now resolves those bindings
  once, on an ordinary stack, before it starts its workers
  (`stack::probe::warm_unwinder`).
* **Bug: A1's at-return check.** The check expected a completed read to hold
  the round's data byte, but a read that a cancel could not stop (`-EALREADY`)
  legitimately completes with the late data the harness writes at the round's
  deadline, and returns after it. The first full-scale `cancel-uring` group
  (five repetitions) therefore reported {{ f"{sum(int(r['value']) for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-cancel-uring-before-check-fix/cancel-uring.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'buffer_writes_after_return' and r['status'] == 'failed'):,}" }}
  bytes "after return" in {{ sum(1 for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-cancel-uring-before-check-fix/cancel-uring.csv')) if r['metric'] == 'buffer_writes_after_return' and r['status'] == 'failed') }}
  runs, all at return and none afterwards (`bad_late=0`), each in a run whose
  slowest cancellation took about 30 s. Late bytes within the length the read
  itself returned now count as data it delivered (`completed_with_late_data`),
  and the group was rerun. The superseded rows are kept in
  `results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/superseded-cancel-uring-before-check-fix/`,
  outside `summarize.py`'s reach. The fix changed only
  `src/bench/cancel_uring.rs`: `versions.txt` shows the sources' digest
  `789d610d...` for deep-recursion and idle-memory (and the superseded first
  runs of spawn-join and cancel-uring), `51e42157...` for max-tasks and the
  cancel-uring rerun (and the superseded first runs of starvation,
  probe-overhead and echo), and `6a1fedf5...` for the gated groups, whose
  sources differ from `51e42157...` only in the scripts (`load.sh`, `run_all.sh`,
  `controls/run_controls.sh`): their `versions.txt` entries record the binary's
  digest, `5893a273...`, which is the binary cargo built from `51e42157...` at
  20:50 UTC for max-tasks and did not rebuild afterwards.
* **New measurements for the registered protocol.** `idle-memory
  --skip-excursion` (policy 7); `max-tasks --hold-secs 60` (section 6.5's
  60-second survival); `cancel-uring --pipe-mix cancel` with 4 KiB buffers and
  the cancel-to-return latency (A1 as registered); `deep-recursion --framed`
  (256-byte frames with drop code in every frame); the `loop` workload of
  `probe-overhead` (policy 8); the SMT state, threads per core and hypervisor in
  `machine-spec`. The Go and Java controls take `--skip-excursion` and
  `--hold-secs` too.
* **The machine's load** (`load.sh`, sourced by `run_all.sh` and the controls):
  every benchmark process gets a row in `load.csv` with its wall-clock and CPU
  time and the CPU time the other processes used meanwhile (`/proc/stat`'s busy
  time of all CPUs, interrupts and softirqs included, less the process's own),
  and `--quiet-below CPUS` makes each process wait for a quiet machine and
  measures it again when the others were busy while it ran (the last of three
  attempts is kept whatever its load; discarded attempts keep their `load.csv`
  row and lose their CSV rows). `summarize.py` prints the load table of
  section 11. The gate is off by default, and off in the M2 workflow.
* **Scripts.** `scale.sh` holds both scales' parameters for `run_all.sh` and the
  controls; `run_all.sh --reps N` (five at full scale) runs repetitions in
  rep-major order with each benchmark's controls right after it, and writes
  `timing.csv` and a record of every invocation in `versions.txt` (with a digest
  of the sources and of the binary); the controls run Go's starvation with its default preemption
  only, and record a connection count beyond the hard limit as a failed row.
  `summarize.py` reports medians and ranges over runs, bootstrap intervals, the
  policies' baselines and section 2.4's rule.

### 10.4 Not run, and why

| Cell | Machine | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Echo at 10k connections | M1 | both ends in one process need 2 x 10,000 + 64 = 20,064 descriptors; the hard `RLIMIT_NOFILE` is 20000 and cannot be raised (policy 3). A client in a second process would fit, and is not implemented |
| Idle connections at 100k | M1 | the same limit; run at 9,900, the most it allows |
| 1M tasks after the excursion for A, B, C and Go | M1 | policy 7; about 33 GiB |
| Everything at full scale | M2 | pending the `harness-run-*` tag; see 10.2 for what a 2-vCPU, 8 GB runner can reproduce |
| Java | M2 (smoke) | JDK 17 on the runner image; the full-scale workflow installs JDK 21 |
| Instruction counts for probe overhead | M1, M2 | no PMU on either machine |
| The semantics rows, the `sim` study, the corpus stack-depth histogram, interpreter starvation | both | interpreter era (Phases 6-9), not Phase 1 |

### 10.5 Measured on one machine only

Every full-scale cell (100k and 1M tasks, five runs, 1M cancelled reads, the
60-second hold, idle connections at 9,900, starvation with four tasks per
worker, the framed recursion, the back-edge yield check) is from M1 alone.

## 11. How the runs were made

On M1, each benchmark group was one invocation, all into the same directory:

```
./run_all.sh --scale full --reps 5 --machine-id vm --out results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1 --only <benchmark> [--quiet-below 0.3]
```

M1 is a development VM, and other work (compilations and tests elsewhere in the
repository) ran on it at times that were not recorded. No two benchmark
processes ran at once. The groups ran in this order: deep-recursion,
spawn-join, idle-memory, cancel-uring, max-tasks, cancel-uring again (after the
fix of A1's check), starvation, probe-overhead and echo; then, because the
first runs of the four groups that measure time had shared the VM with that
work (section 10.2), spawn-join, probe-overhead, starvation and echo again,
and idle-connections, all five with `--quiet-below 0.3`: before every process
the script waited until the other processes had used fewer than 0.3 CPUs over
2 s, and it measured a process again (up to three attempts) when they had used
0.3 CPUs or more on average while it ran, and at least 0.05 CPU seconds in all
(five clock ticks, so that a process of a few milliseconds is not measured
again for one stray tick; `load.sh`). Within a group the
repetitions run in rep-major order (every configuration and its controls once,
then again). The idle-connections group's runner was stopped by a two-hour limit
on background jobs in the environment that ran it, during the fifth repetition
and after the harness's seven configurations; that repetition's Go and Java
runs were then made by hand with the same gate (`controls/run_controls.sh ...
--only idle-connections --no-build --quiet-below 0.3`, described in
`aborted-gated-rerun/why-aborted.txt`), so `timing.csv` has four repetitions of
that group and every cell has five runs. Wall-clock time per group, from the `timing.csv` files of the
results directory and its subdirectories:

{{ table("timing") }}

The machine's load during every process of the gated groups, from `load.csv`
(the other processes' CPU time is all CPUs' busy time from `/proc/stat` less
the process's own; it can be a few ticks below zero):

{{ table("load_summary") }}

The other work came in bursts: the gate measured a process again
{{ sum(1 for r in __import__('csv').DictReader(open(TB + '/results/vm/2026-10-13-phase1/load.csv')) if r['kept'] == 'no') }}
times. Four processes were kept on their third attempt although the other
processes had used more than 0.3 CPUs while they ran (the last attempt is kept
whatever its load): two starvation runs at K = 10k (C and D(A)) and two echo
runs at 1000 connections (A and C). The two echo runs have the highest p99 of
their cells, and the two starvation runs lie within their cells' ranges, far
under the threshold; none of these cells decides a verdict.

M2's full run is `.github/workflows/harness-full.yml`, triggered by pushing a
tag `harness-run-*`; it runs `./run_all.sh --scale full --machine-id
github-runner --out results/github-runner/full-<tag>` without the gate (a
hosted runner runs nothing else) and uploads `taskbench/results/github-runner/`
as the artifact `taskbench-full-<tag>`.
